  "reasoning": "The patient presents with three key symptoms: trouble breathing during exercise, excessive sweating/getting hot easily, and frequent yeast infections. While 'trouble breathing when I exercise' could initially suggest bronchial asthma, and 'yeast infections' might suggest a localized fungal infection, neither isolated condition explains the full clinical picture. Recurrent yeast infections are a classic hallmark of diabetes mellitus, as elevated blood glucose levels create an environment that promotes yeast overgrowth. Excessive sweating and heat intolerance frequently result from autonomic neuropathy or metabolic dysregulation associated with diabetes. Additionally, exertional dyspnea (trouble breathing on exercise) commonly stems from cardiovascular complications or general deconditioning seen in diabetic patients. Diabetes is the unifying systemic diagnosis that accounts for all three symptoms together.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]diabetes[/DIAGNOSIS]"
}
